3 Government and Politics
in Hong Kong: A Colonial
Paradox
Steve Tsang
Government and politics in British Hong Kong are a paradox. Constitutionally the political system is that of a Crown Colony created in the Victorian era, which had remained little changed until a decade ago. Before the concessions were made in the mid-1980s to allow for a limited degree of public representation and participation, it was an entirely autocratic system. It provided near-absolute power to its head, the Governor, who was appointed by and answerable only to the British Government almost half-way across the world. Nevertheless, the British administration of Hong Kong had managed to fulfil by the early 1980s the requirements of 'as good a government as practicable' in the political tradition not of the British Empire-Commonwealth but of China. This is an achievement which hardly any Chinese government has achieved at least since the days of Confucius (c.551-479 BC). The focus of this chapter is to seek an explanation for this paradox by examining the nature of British rule in Hong Kong. It begins with a short explanation of the requirements of 'as good a government as practicable' in the Chinese political tradition. Then follows critical analysis of the founding and evolution of the British administration in Hong Kong in its first century. It goes on to highlight the impact of the Pacific War on the nature of the colonial government, and examines how all the requirements came to be fulfilled by the early 1980s. It ends with a different paradox: having met the requirements, in the mid-1980s the Hong Kong Government found itself at risk of falling short of local expectations again, after Britain and China agreed that the territory should be returned to Chinese rule in 1997.
