'THE BEST POSSIBLE GOVERNMENT' IN THE CHINESE POLITICAL TRADITION
It is beyond the scope of this chapter to engage in a discourse on the sources of the Chinese political tradition or the intellectual origins of the ideal government in this tradition. For our purposes, it can be argued that the mainstay of the Chinese political tradition is Confucianism as modified over the ages. The proper way to govern as explained by Confucius in The Analects is to 'uphold and promote the five virtues and suppress the four evils'. 1 As the basis for good government in the Confucian view is centred on the gentleman-official, he explained that the five virtues were for the gentleman-official to bring to the people benefits instead of drudgery; to work for the people without complaint; to pursue the right cause and not succumb to advancing self-interest; to promote prosperity without becoming arrogant; and to maintain dignity and authority without being harsh.:! The four evils, in turn, were defined by Confucius as enforcing the death penalty without first providing proper education to the people; demanding immediate results without giving due notice; penalising others for one's negligence; and withholding payment when or where it was due.3 Mencius added that governments exist 'for the sake of the governed, to give the people peace and sufficiency, and to lead them by education and example to the life of virtue' .4 The ideal government in the Confucian tradition would be onc constituted by gentlemen and headed by a sage-king, which would meet all the requirements outlined above. Such a government, however, has not existed in the recorded history of China.5 In the absence of this ideal, what the Chinese looked for was a government constituted by upright Confucian gentlemen-officials who were expected to act like wise and benevolent paternal figures to the people. The act of government itself, however, was seen by the common people in terms not dissimilar to garbage collection. It was an essential job which had to be done efficiently and effectively, but it was also one which should preferably be done out of sight. In other words, the best government which seems practicable is one which is efficient, fair, honest and paternalistic, but at the same time non-intrusive into the lives of the ordinary people.


