FOUNDATIONS OF THE HONG KONG GOVERNMENT
Hong Kong was not founded by the British as a Crown Colony to serve the interests of China, even less as a political experiment to test the practicality of the ideal government in the Chinese political tradition. It was founded in the 1840s to serve British interests, which were described by WiIliam Gladstone (Secretary of State for the Colonies, 1845-46) as 'solely and exclusively ... commercial'.6 While the advancement of the interests of the Chinese Empire or of the Chinese inhabitants there was not part of the British intention, the British also had no objection should their occupation of Hong Kong also prove advantageous to the Chinese. In time it did. A" British Hong Kong developed, it gradually came to be seen by those Chinese who migrated there and by the more reformminded Chinese as serving them well. The first group voted for Hong Kong with their feet, as this alien enclave provided them with stability, good order and a congenial socio-political environment to enrich themselves. To the latter group, Hong Kong was actually a source of inspiration and a model for China. Sun Yat-sen, generally accepted by the Chinese as the pioneer of their republican revolution, admitted that his 'ideas for the revolution had come entirely from Hong Kong'.' In 1923, he publicly paid tribute to the British for their achievements in turning a 'barren island' into modern Hong Kong in seven to eight decades, something which China had failed to achieve in its four thousand years of civilisation. 8 Nonetheless, if British rule has turned out to be positive and acceptable to the Chinese of Hong Kong it was, at least in its formative years, not designed for this end. When Hong Kong was founded as a colony it was meant to be the base for supporting British trade with China.9 In the mid-nineteenth century it had to be a naval station and a military base as well. IO From the outset it was an outpost of the British Empire and was never meant to be a settlement colony.ll It was created a