not disrupted by such legislation. Active intrusion by the colonial government was also limited by its own small size and the aloofness of many of its senior officials.20 Moreover, except during the First World War the Hong Kong Government levied no income tax and, apart from a short time in 1941, imposed no immigration controls on its border with China in the first century of its rule.21 It also organised only a haphazard registration of births and made no attempt to direct the economy. Even when the government, from the local Chinese point of view, became intrusive over sanitary matters towards the end of the nineteenth century as a plague was beginning to spread, it was simply responding to a pressing health problem.22 In reality, most Chinese residents in the first one hundred years of British rule had little to do with the government except for occasional encounters with government functionaries involved in postal, sanitary or similar services. Those who had had a brush with the law or had suffered some misfortune might have had to deal with the police, or district watchmen, or the fire service or the government medical service. One may question whether this policy of non-interference was maintained in the mid-1920s when nationalism emerged as a major political force in China. In 1925 the British became a target of Chinese nationalism during the 'May Thirtieth Incident' in Shanghai, during which the Britishcommanded municipal police in the International Settlement opened fire on some Chinese demonstrators. In response, a 'strike-boycott' by workers in Hong Kong was organised and enforced by the Chinese Communists, who were at that time allied to the nationalistic Guomindang (Kuomintang) which formed the government of Canton.23 While the rise of nationalism in China forced the British Government to review its policy towards South China generally, the 'strike-boycott' had more limited effect on the policy of the Hong Kong Government.24 As the Guomindang launched its Northern Expedition to unify the country in 1926 it re-directed the force of Chinese nationalism to the north. Once Canton stopped enforcing the 'strike-boycott', Chinese workers of Hong Kong returned to work. As a result the Hong Kong Government did not alter its policy of leaving the local Chinese alone, though it did actively locate and expel


