Communist activists from the Colony. On the whole British Hong Kong had a record of non-intrusion not rivalled by any government in the recorded history of China. The efficiency requirement was also met by the Hong Kong Government from its foundation. The administration which the British established in 1841 was small and simple but was efficient for what was required of it. For a people used to a system where formal government stopped at the xian or county level,25 who were generally not demanding of the government, there was little which the local Chinese had expected of the government that was not handled with reasonable expedition before the Pacific War. After the war, public expectations in Hong Kong rose, but the colonial administration also expanded in size and in scope often in response to public demands. A Resettlement Department, for example, was created in 1954 when the Government started to provide public housing to victims of disasters and the destitute. The administration also became increasingly more sophisticated, as it recruited specialists and professionals to assist its general administrators. It continued to earn a reputation of being both efficient and effective. As far as fairness is concerned, the local Chinese came to feel satisfied well before the centenary of British Hong Kong. There is no question that the colonial government in the last century had adopted summary measures against the Chinese, for example, by cutting off their queues - a major public humiliation at that time - for minor offences.26 But this compared well with what the Chinese would have expected from officialdom in their own country. Notwithstanding racial and other prejudices of that time, to which magistrates and judges were as susceptible as other human beings, the British courts generally treated the Chinese brought in front of them according to the law. If the alien nature of the British legal system provoked negative Chinese reactions at first, in time 'the English s