THE ULTIMATE IRONY
It is ironic that after the early 1980s when the British government of Hong Kong had lived up to the requirements of the Chinese political tradition, it took only a few years to find itself at risk of falling short of local public expectations. By that time a distinct Hong Kong political culture had emerged and local expectations had changed.77 Even meeting the standard of the ideal government in the Chinese tradition was not then enough. The people of Hong Kong increasingly wanted democracy. The track record of the colonial government and the British Government's 1984 declaration to 'build up a firmly-based, democratic administration in Hong Kong in the years between now and 1997'78 suggest this local demand would have been accommodated, if it had been a matter for the British to decide. It was not. The restraining factor has been the Chinese government. Deng Xiaoping, the paramount Chinese leader, made it clear that democracy involving direct elections in Hong Kong was objectionable as it would not guarantee the return of candidates acceptable to the Chinese government.79 The Chinese Government representative in Hong Kong, Xu Jiatun, put such views vigorously. In November 1985 he made this point forcefully at a press conference, and reiterated it privately to the Governor's unofficial advisers.Ho Within a month, the British agreed to the Chinese demand, accepting that political reform under the British would dovetail with the Basic Law for the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region. At that time, the drafting of the Basic Law had just started, and would not be completed until 1990.H1 In the second half of the 1980s the


