process of democratisation was slowed down while public demand for it continued to grow. It is paradoxical that it has taken British imperial rule in Hong Kong to deliver a government which meets the requirements of 'as good a government as possible in the Chinese tradition'. However, the ul timate irony must be that such a government began to fall short of the expectations of the local Chinese when the direction and pace of political change came to be dominated by the Central People's Government of the People's Republic of China.
